{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfdCB2uAs9vF69ZKY4S6G","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote the candidate, and the self-check printed `OK`. It scored an ELPD-LOO of −3493.1 on the short smoke fit, against −3500.3 for the incumbent. That number isn't a fair comparison: the smoke fit was 100 draws on one chain, and its PSIS-LOO came out unreliable (8.7% of trials had Pareto k > 0.5; the tolerance is 1%). Convergence and novelty weren't checked; admission's full fit will test both.\n\n**Model name:** `personal_overalternation_leniency`\n\n**What it refines:** the incumbent, `asymmetric_tolerance_lopsided_stretch`. In the incumbent, switching more often than your ideal rate costs only a fraction of what switching too rarely costs, and that fraction is shared by everyone.\n\n**The one change:** the fraction is now each person's own trait, drawn from a population. Some people are lenient toward sequences that switch too often; others penalise them as hard as streaky ones. The model predicts that people disagree most on pairs of highly alternating sequences, more than their different ideal rates alone would produce. Everything else is unchanged: the most lopsided stretch, the ideal rate and sensitivity, imbalance, longest streak, final streak, mirror symmetry, run-length variety and the guessing rate.\n\n**Why this rather than the critique's side bias:** the critique's only discrepancy, people differing in how often they press Left, doesn't survive the FDR correction (q = 0.128). Side-bias versions of the parent model also gained less than one nat over it (rank 2 at −3530.6 against the parent at −3531.2), so another one seemed unlikely to help.\n\n**Risks at admission:**\n- **Convergence:** the change adds 400 person-level parameters, so the full fit's convergence gate is the main risk.\n- **Novelty:** the gate compares predictions averaged over participants. If spreading the fraction across people barely moves those averages, the candidate could fall under the 0.002 threshold against the incumbent.\n\nThe three files are in `experiment3/model_loop/iter_1/candidate_4/`: `hypothesis.md`, `model_name.txt` and `candidate.py`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2770,"cache_read_input_tokens":81804,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2770,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":701,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c2f58116-cee5-4dbf-81bd-f90ae175a50b","uuid":"34a310e4-5708-4bc9-b96f-0b8fcc2cad29","timestamp":"2026-10-02T09:43:28.161Z","request_id":"req_011CfdCB2UdBAwCVsC2itUCM"}
